12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine the 'Aesthetic-Usability Effect' discovered by Masaaki Kurosu and Kaori Kashimura. How does high visual polish deceive usability evaluators during prototype testing?) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Kurosu and Kashimura's Usability Experiment \(1995\):) Tj
T*
(   - Researchers presented participants with 26 layout variations of ATM automated teller) Tj
T*
(   interfaces.) Tj
T*
(   - All variations shared identical operational buttons and functional logic, but varied) Tj
T*
(   systematically in their aesthetic beauty \(visual balance, button styling, symmetry\).) Tj
T*
(   - The Discovery: Participants consistently rated the aesthetically beautiful interfaces as) Tj
T*
(   significantly easier to use, both before AND after completing complex operational tasks!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Psychological Mechanisms:) Tj
T*
(   - High visual polish induces a positive affective emotional state in the user's brain.) Tj
T*
(   - Positive affect broadens cognitive flexibility and increases tolerance for minor interface) Tj
T*
(   friction; users blame themselves for mistakes rather than the software \('I must have clicked) Tj
T*
ET

