(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the 'Cardboard-and-Duct-Tape' prototyping methodology for hardware-software interactive devices. How did IDEO use this to design the first surgical handheld wand?) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Rapid Physical-Digital Prototyping:) Tj
T*
(   - When designing physical devices with embedded software controls, 3D CAD modeling and) Tj
T*
(   injection-molded plastics take weeks and thousands of dollars to fabricate.) Tj
T*
(   - Cardboard, foam, duct tape, and hot glue allow engineering teams to sculpt physical form) Tj
T*
(   factors in hours, evaluating ergonomic grasp, button reachability, and weight distribution.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. IDEO's Surgical Wand Case Study \(David Kelley\):) Tj
T*
(   - The Challenge: Surgeons required a handheld ultrasonic surgical wand with embedded control) Tj
T*
(   buttons to navigate internal tissue during surgery.) Tj
T*
(   - The Rapid Prototype: In a brainstorming session with surgeons, IDEO designers grabbed a) Tj
T*
(   plastic whiteboard marker, an empty plastic film canister, a clothespin, and secured them with) Tj
T*
(   masking tape.) Tj
T*
(   - Real-Time Ergonomic Discovery:) Tj
T*
(   * Kelley handed the makeshift marker assembly to a surgeon: 'Hold it like you are operating.') Tj
T*
(   * The surgeon immediately adjusted their grip, demonstrating that they held the instrument like) Tj
T*
(   a delicate pencil, not a heavy hammer!) Tj
T*
(   * The designer moved the clothespin \(button trigger\) 2 inches forward to sit directly beneath) Tj
T*
(   the surgeon's index fingertip.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Engineering Impact:) Tj
T*
(   - In 15 minutes and for zero dollars, the team established the definitive physical button) Tj
T*
(   placement and ergonomic contour, avoiding a $100,000 plastic tooling mistake.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
