(   [Problem Statement]: Evaluate the 'Paper Prototype Facilitator Neutrality' principle. How do subtle unconscious verbal cues from a testing facilitator invalidate experimental usability data?) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Fragility of Qualitative Usability Data:) Tj
T*
(   - Usability testing evaluates human-system communication. Because humans are intensely attuned) Tj
T*
(   to social hierarchy and approval, the facilitator holds enormous psychological authority over) Tj
T*
(   the participant.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Subconscious Sabotage Mechanisms:) Tj
T*
(   - Verbal Affirmation Bias: When a user clicks the correct paper button, the facilitator says) Tj
T*
(   'Good!' or 'Great!'. The user is conditioned to seek praise, turning the test into a game of) Tj
T*
(   guessing what pleases the researcher.) Tj
T*
(   - Defensive Rationalization: When a user hesitates at an ambiguous icon, the facilitator) Tj
T*
(   interjects: 'Remember, you are trying to find settings.' This artificial hint obscures the fact) Tj
T*
(   that the icon is uninterpretable in the wild.) Tj
T*
(   - Non-Verbal Leakage: Leaning forward, gasping, or smiling when the user approaches the) Tj
T*
(   intended button provides subconscious navigational breadcrumbs.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Strict Neutrality Protocols:) Tj
T*
(   - The Echo Technique: When a user asks 'Should I click this?', reflect: 'What would you expect) Tj
T*
(   to happen if you clicked that?') Tj
T*
(   - Monotone Affirmation: Replace 'Good job!' with neutral acknowledgments: 'I see what you did;) Tj
T*
(   please keep talking aloud.') Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
