(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the '80/20 Rule' \(Pareto Principle\) in Minimum Viable Product scoping. How does an agile engineering team identify the 20% of features that deliver 80% of customer value?) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Pareto Dynamics in Software Systems:) Tj
T*
(   - Research across enterprise software platforms \(e.g., Standish Group studies\) consistently) Tj
T*
(   reveals that 64% of developed software features are RARELY or NEVER used by customers, while) Tj
T*
(   20% of core features generate over 80% of total daily active usage.) Tj
T*
(   - Traditional engineering projects waste millions building the 80% unused long-tail features.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Identifying the Critical 20% Core:) Tj
T*
(   - Step 1: User Job-To-Be-Done \(JTBD\) Mapping: Dissect the core primary workflow \(e.g., 'Share a) Tj
T*
(   file with a coworker'\).) Tj
T*
(   - Step 2: Critical Path Elimination: Strip away all peripheral features \(file tagging, custom) Tj
T*
(   color themes, activity analytics, third-party integrations\). If the user cannot complete the) Tj
T*
(   file upload and link generation, the product fails.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 3: Telemetry Analysis of Prototypes: Observe early testers. Identify which 2-3 actions) Tj
T*
(   are executed repeatedly while other tabs gather dust.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Scoping the MVP:) Tj
T*
(   - Build ONLY the critical path with exceptional speed, stability, and zero friction. Delay) Tj
T*
(   everything else to post-launch iterations based on actual customer pull.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
