(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze how 'Rapid Prototyping' accelerates compliance validation in medical device software engineering \(FDA 21 CFR Part 820.30 Design Controls\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Regulatory Waterfall Myth:) Tj
T*
(   - Traditional medical device engineering misinterprets FDA regulations as requiring a rigid,) Tj
T*
(   documentation-heavy waterfall lifecycle, leading to multi-year development cycles before) Tj
T*
(   clinical usability is evaluated.) Tj
T*
(   - FDA 21 CFR 820.30 and IEC 62366 explicitly mandate Human Factors Engineering \(HFE\) and) Tj
T*
(   usability validation to identify use-related safety hazards early.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Integrating Rapid Prototyping into Design Controls:) Tj
T*
(   - Phase 1: User Needs Identification \(Design Inputs\): Rapid low-fidelity prototyping with) Tj
T*
(   clinical nurses exposes cognitive ambiguities and medication dosing risks during concept) Tj
T*
(   formation.) Tj
T*
(   - Phase 2: Formative Usability Testing: Interactive wireframes and simulated hardware rigs are) Tj
T*
(   evaluated in simulated hospital environments, discovering safety hazards \(use errors\) before) Tj
T*
(   the design freezes.) Tj
T*
(   - Phase 3: Traceability & Risk Mitigation: Each discovered prototype usability defect is) Tj
T*
(   documented in the Design History File \(DHF\) alongside its engineering countermeasure,) Tj
T*
(   satisfying FDA design verification requirements empirically.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Clinical Impact:) Tj
T*
(   - Prevents fatal medical dosing errors, reduces post-market recall risks, and slashes total FDA) Tj
T*
(   regulatory clearance timelines by over 40%.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
